<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Permits plain-HTTP (cleartext) traffic.

  Required, not a shortcut: Spoolman is a self-hosted service that in practice runs over plain HTTP on
  a LAN (`http://spoolman.local:7912`, a Home Assistant add-on, a Docker host…), and it has no TLS of
  its own. Since `targetSdk` is 28 or higher, Android blocks cleartext by default, so without this the
  app cannot reach a typical Spoolman at all — the failure looks identical to an unreachable server,
  which is exactly how it presented during testing.

  Not scoped to specific domains because the server address is entered by the user at runtime and is
  unknown at build time; there is no set of hostnames to enumerate here.

  The trade-off is accepted deliberately: the only traffic this app makes is to the user's own Spoolman
  instance, the data is a filament inventory, and Spoolman has no credentials to intercept
  (DESIGN.md DEC-06, "Spoolman needs no credentials"). If a user fronts their instance with HTTPS, an `https://` address works
  unchanged and gets TLS — this permits cleartext, it does not force it.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
